(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-4-physiology-master-exam-form-d"]={"title":"Physiology Comprehensive Master Exam — Form D","category":"Physiology","questions":[{"q":"How does growth hormone inhibit carbohydrate utilization?","choices":["By increasing glucose uptake in both muscle and fat, and lowering hepatic output","By stimulating pancreatic insulin release exclusively, with no direct hepatic effect","By decreasing glucose uptake in muscle and fat, and increasing hepatic gluconeogenesis","By directly destroying circulating glucose molecules through oxidative enzymatic breakdown"],"answer":2,"exp":"GH decreases glucose uptake in both muscle and fat, while increasing hepatic gluconeogenesis — together reducing carbohydrate utilization. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Glucagon's actions, which oppose insulin, include increased glycogenolysis and:","choices":["No effect on glucose production","Increased gluconeogenesis","Increased glycogen synthesis","Decreased gluconeogenesis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Glucagon's actions include increased glycogenolysis AND increased gluconeogenesis, both raising plasma glucose, opposing insulin. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Placental estrogen is synthesized primarily from which precursor?","choices":["Maternal cholesterol, converted directly without intermediate steps","Androgens (DHEA, 16-hydroxy-DHEA) from maternal and fetal adrenal sources","Progesterone produced earlier by the maternal corpus luteum","Thyroxine released from the maternal thyroid gland into circulation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Placental estrogen is synthesized primarily from androgens (DHEA and 16-hydroxy-DHEA), sourced from both maternal and fetal adrenal glands (with a large fetal adrenal cortex producing DHEA). — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"Under normal conditions, glomerular hydrostatic pressure (PG) is approximately:","choices":["60 mmHg","120 mmHg","10 mmHg","200 mmHg"],"answer":0,"exp":"Under normal conditions, glomerular hydrostatic pressure is approximately 60 mmHg. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What do the thin limbs of the loop of Henle utilize to facilitate transport?","choices":["ATP-driven active transport exclusively","The medulla's osmolality","Hormonal signals from the anterior pituitary","Direct innervation by the vagus nerve"],"answer":1,"exp":"The thin limbs utilize the medulla's osmolality (the corticomedullary gradient) to facilitate transport, rather than relying on active transport. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"The speed of contraction of a skeletal muscle fiber is determined by the maximum velocity (Vmax) of which enzyme?","choices":["Acetylcholinesterase (AChE)","Myosin ATPase","Myosin light chain kinase (MLCK)","Creatine kinase"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the slides state contraction speed is set by the Vmax of myosin ATPase. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 31"},{"q":"The two major hormones of the posterior pituitary are ADH and:","choices":["Oxytocin","ACTH","Prolactin","TSH"],"answer":0,"exp":"ADH (vasopressin) and oxytocin are the two major posterior pituitary hormones. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Aldosterone is produced in which zone of the adrenal cortex?","choices":["Zona fasciculata","Adrenal medulla","Zona glomerulosa","Zona reticularis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Aldosterone is produced in the zona glomerulosa, the outermost zone of the adrenal cortex. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"During capacitation, what happens to the sperm's acrosome that enables penetration of the ovum?","choices":["It contains hyaluronidase and proteolytic enzymes used for penetration","It becomes completely impermeable to extracellular calcium ions","It dissolves completely and is lost before fertilization","It converts to a structure resembling the trophoblast"],"answer":0,"exp":"The acrosome contains hyaluronidase and proteolytic enzymes that enable the sperm to penetrate the ovum. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What is the basic equation for GFR?","choices":["GFR = Renal blood flow ÷ filtration fraction","GFR = Cardiac output × renal fraction","GFR = Tubular reabsorption − tubular secretion","GFR = Kf × Net Filtration Pressure"],"answer":3,"exp":"GFR equals the glomerular filtration coefficient (Kf) multiplied by the net filtration pressure across the glomerular capillaries. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"In the 'Fluid Displacement' step of the countercurrent multiplier, water moves out of the thin descending limb because of:","choices":["Active transport of water directly by the descending limb epithelium itself","The higher sodium concentration in the interstitium created by the ascending limb","The active secretion of potassium directly into the descending limb lumen","Aquaporin-2 channel insertion triggered by circulating ADH hormone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Water is drawn out of the water-permeable thin descending limb by the higher sodium concentration in the interstitium (created by the ascending limb's active sodium transport). — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"By what mechanism does parathyroid hormone (PTH) increase osteoclast formation?","choices":["By increasing osteoprotegerin (OPG) so that RANKL is released","By stimulating osteoblasts to produce RANKL and decreasing osteoprotegerin (OPG)","By binding receptors directly on mature osteoclasts to activate their resorption","By converting growth-plate chondrocytes into mature osteoclasts"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: PTH makes osteoblasts form RANKL and release M-CSF while lowering OPG, promoting osteoclast differentiation. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 63"},{"q":"Corticotropes secrete ACTH, which stimulates secretion of which substances from the adrenal cortex?","choices":["Mineralocorticoids exclusively","Catecholamines only","Glucocorticoids and androgens","Thyroid hormones"],"answer":2,"exp":"ACTH stimulates secretion of glucocorticoids and androgens from the adrenal cortex. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"The adrenal medulla is grouped under which broad hormone class in this lecture's overview, distinct from the adrenal cortex?","choices":["Peptide hormones exclusively, unrelated to amines","Eicosanoids","Steroid hormones","Amine hormones"],"answer":3,"exp":"The adrenal medulla's hormones are grouped under the amine hormone class ('Adrenal Medullary Hormones—Amine'), distinct from the adrenal cortex's steroid hormones. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"How many umbilical arteries and umbilical veins are described in normal placental circulation, and what does each carry?","choices":["2 arteries (deoxygenated from fetus to villi), 1 vein (oxygenated from villi to fetus)","1 artery and 1 vein, with both vessels carrying fully oxygenated blood","1 artery (oxygenated blood to fetus), 2 veins (deoxygenated blood from fetus)","2 arteries and 2 veins, with all four vessels carrying identically mixed blood"],"answer":0,"exp":"There are 2 umbilical arteries carrying deoxygenated blood from the fetus to the villi, and 1 umbilical vein carrying oxygenated blood from the villi back to the fetus. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"According to the lecture, changes in which pressure are the primary means of physiological regulation of GFR?","choices":["Bowman's capsule hydrostatic pressure (PB)","Glomerular colloid osmotic pressure (πG) alone","Glomerular hydrostatic pressure (PG)","Central venous pressure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Changes in glomerular hydrostatic pressure (PG) are the primary means of physiologic regulation of GFR — an increase raises GFR, a decrease reduces it. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Roughly what percentage of filtered HCO3- does the proximal tubule reabsorb?","choices":["~40-50%","~80-90%","~10-20%","~100%, with none left for other segments"],"answer":1,"exp":"The proximal tubule reabsorbs approximately 80-90% of filtered HCO3-, a process that requires carbonic anhydrase. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which structure is an example of MULTIUNITARY smooth muscle?","choices":["The muscular wall of the gut","The muscular wall of the airways","The iris of the eye","The wall of a blood vessel"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the iris, along with the vas deferens and piloerectors, is listed as multiunitary. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the listed mechanisms of hormone clearance?","choices":["Active reabsorption by the thyroid gland","Binding to tissue receptors","Metabolic destruction (endocytosis, enzymes)","Excretion by the kidneys into urine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Active reabsorption by the thyroid gland is not one of the listed hormone clearance mechanisms. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which neural influence stimulates glucagon secretion via acetylcholine?","choices":["Enteric interneurons exclusively","Somatic motor neurons","Sympathetic activity","Vagal activity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Vagal activity, acting through acetylcholine, is listed as a neural influence that stimulates glucagon secretion (alongside sympathetic/adrenergic stimulation). — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"What triggers ovulation and development of the corpus luteum?","choices":["A drop in FSH to zero","Progesterone acting alone, with no LH involvement","A steady, unchanging level of estrogen","An LH surge"],"answer":3,"exp":"An LH surge leads to ovulation and development of the corpus luteum. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A single complete micturition reflex cycle includes which sequence of pressure changes?","choices":["A progressive and rapid increase of pressure, a period of sustained pressure, then a return to basal tone","A period of initially sustained pressure, then a slow gradual decline, then one final pressure spike.","An immediate return to basal tone with no rise in pressure or sustained phase at all.","A single instantaneous pressure spike occurring with absolutely no other distinct phases."],"answer":0,"exp":"A single complete cycle includes a progressive and rapid increase of pressure, a period of sustained pressure, and then a return of pressure to the basal tone of the bladder. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"The vasa recta, as a countercurrent exchanger, functions to:","choices":["Actively transport sodium ions directly out of the tubular fluid","Create the corticomedullary gradient from scratch, independent of the loop","Maintain the corticomedullary gradient while supplying blood to the medulla","Serve as the primary anatomical site of ADH hormone secretion"],"answer":2,"exp":"The vasa recta maintains (preserves) the corticomedullary gradient established by the loop of Henle, while still supplying blood to the medulla. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"In skeletal muscle, the triad is defined as the junction between which structures?","choices":["Two T-tubules flanking one terminal cisterna","A thick filament, a thin filament, and titin","The sarcolemma, a mitochondrion, and a myofibril","Two terminal cisternae flanking one T-tubule"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the triad is the junction of two terminal cisternae of the sarcoplasmic reticulum with one T-tubule. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which neurotransmitter class is described as increasing GH secretion via hypothalamic control?","choices":["Catecholamines","Serotonin exclusively","GABA","Acetylcholine exclusively"],"answer":0,"exp":"Catecholamines are described as increasing GH secretion as part of the hypothalamic control pathway (via the ventromedial nucleus/GHRH). — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Besides insulin and glucagon, which other hormones does the pancreas produce, per this lecture's overview?","choices":["Amylin, somatostatin, and pancreatic polypeptide","Cortisol, aldosterone, and adrenal androgen hormones","PTH, calcitonin, and calcitriol (active vitamin D)","TSH, ACTH, prolactin, and growth hormone"],"answer":0,"exp":"The pancreas also produces amylin, somatostatin, and pancreatic polypeptide, alongside insulin and glucagon. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Progesterone's effect on the endometrium is best described as promoting:","choices":["No measurable effect on the endometrium at any stage of the cycle","Progressive atrophy of the endometrial lining over the cycle","Complete immediate shedding of the endometrium with no secretory phase","Secretory changes in the endometrium (the endometrial/menstrual cycle)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Progesterone promotes secretory changes in the endometrium, driving the endometrial (menstrual) cycle. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"How does an individual voluntarily initiate urination even before the bladder reaches its natural reflex threshold?","choices":["By voluntarily contracting abdominal muscles to raise bladder pressure and stretch the bladder neck/posterior urethra, exciting the reflex, while relaxing the external sphincter","By voluntarily and forcefully constricting both ureters near the bladder wall to push urine in ahead of the natural filling schedule.","There is no voluntary mechanism whatsoever that allows a person to initiate urination before the natural reflex threshold is reached.","By completely and permanently inhibiting all afferent and efferent signaling carried through the pelvic nerve pathway to the bladder wall."],"answer":0,"exp":"Voluntarily contracting the abdominal muscles increases bladder pressure, forcing urine into the bladder neck/posterior urethra, which stretches and stimulates stretch receptors to excite the micturition reflex, while the external sphincter is simultaneously relaxed. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What happens if angiotensin II-mediated efferent arteriole constriction becomes excessive?","choices":["GFR is preserved indefinitely with no downside","The afferent arteriole compensates by constricting further","Renal blood flow increases without limit","GFR can eventually decrease"],"answer":3,"exp":"While mild efferent constriction preserves GFR, excessive constriction can eventually decrease GFR. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"How is neurotransmitter delivered at the smooth-muscle neuromuscular junction?","choices":["By direct cytoplasmic transfer of transmitter through gap junctions","From varicosities in diffuse junctions, diffusing to the muscle cells","Via acetylcholinesterase packaged within the synaptic cleft","Through a single discrete motor end plate located on each fiber"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: autonomic fibers form diffuse junctions whose varicosities release transmitter that diffuses to the cells. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 42"},{"q":"Approximately what percentage of the anterior pituitary is composed of somatotropes?","choices":["About 95%","About 65%","About 35%","About 5%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Somatotropes make up about 35% of the anterior pituitary. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Where is epinephrine produced, per this lecture?","choices":["Thyroid gland","Adrenal cortex","Anterior pituitary","Adrenal medulla"],"answer":3,"exp":"Epinephrine is produced in the adrenal medulla. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Relaxin, secreted from the corpus luteum and stimulated by hCG, has a possible role to:","choices":["Stimulate ovulation","Relax pelvic ligaments","Increase uterine contractility early in pregnancy","Constrict pelvic ligaments to stabilize the pelvis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Relaxin's possible role is to relax pelvic ligaments, in preparation for later stages of pregnancy/delivery. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"During a strong micturition contraction, detrusor contraction raises bladder pressure to approximately:","choices":["40–60 mmHg","5–10 mmHg","100–120 mmHg","200 mmHg or higher"],"answer":0,"exp":"Detrusor contraction increases bladder pressure to about 40–60 mmHg, which is the major step in emptying the bladder. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Where does reabsorption of filtrate first occur as it moves through the nephron?","choices":["Distal convoluted tubule","Thin descending limb","The glomerular capsule region","Proximal convoluted tubule"],"answer":3,"exp":"The proximal convoluted tubule is where filtrate first flows after the glomerular capsule and where reabsorption first occurs, moving material across the apical membrane back into blood. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Smooth muscle is remarkably energy efficient; its oxygen consumption is approximately:","choices":["Twice that of skeletal muscle at the same tension","10% that of skeletal muscle at the same tension","Equal to skeletal muscle at the same tension","1% that of skeletal muscle at the same tension"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the lecture states smooth muscle uses about 1% of the oxygen of an equal weight of skeletal muscle at the same tension. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 41"},{"q":"Posterior pituitary hormones are synthesized in the hypothalamus and transported to nerve terminals via which carrier molecules?","choices":["Neurophysins","Thyroxine-binding globulins","Albumin","Transcortin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Posterior pituitary hormones (ADH, oxytocin) are synthesized in the hypothalamus and transported to nerve terminals via neurophysins. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"The three classes of adrenal cortical hormones are mineralocorticoids, glucocorticoids, and:","choices":["Thyroid hormones","Peptide hormones","Androgenic hormones","Catecholamines"],"answer":2,"exp":"The three adrenal cortical hormone classes are mineralocorticoids, glucocorticoids, and androgenic hormones. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"One function of placental progesterone is to reduce uterine contractility, which helps to:","choices":["Induce labor prematurely","Stimulate immediate lactation","Prevent spontaneous abortion","Increase menstrual flow"],"answer":2,"exp":"Placental progesterone reduces uterine contractility to help prevent spontaneous abortion. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"The high rate of glomerular filtration depends on which two general factors?","choices":["Only the special membrane properties, independent of blood flow","A low rate of blood flow combined with a low glomerular Kf value","Only a high rate of blood flow, independent of any membrane properties at all","A high rate of blood flow plus special properties of the glomerular capillary membranes"],"answer":3,"exp":"The high rate of glomerular filtration depends on both a high rate of blood flow and the special properties of the glomerular capillary membranes. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"ANP's action on the collecting ducts includes inhibiting Na+ reabsorption, which has what effect?","choices":["Decreased Na+ excretion","Increased Na+ excretion","Increased Na+ reabsorption elsewhere that fully compensates","No change in Na+ handling"],"answer":1,"exp":"By inhibiting Na+ reabsorption in the collecting ducts, ANP increases Na+ excretion (natriuresis). — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which statement correctly describes the trigger for sarcoplasmic reticulum calcium release in skeletal muscle?","choices":["Membrane voltage, with release proportional to the membrane voltage","Calcium entry, with release proportional to the amount of calcium that enters","Acetylcholine binding directly to the ryanodine receptor","Mechanical stretch of the terminal cisternae"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: skeletal muscle uses voltage-activated calcium release, and release is proportional to membrane voltage. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which hypothalamic-hypophysial regulatory hormone stimulates ACTH secretion by corticotropes?","choices":["Thyrotropin-releasing hormone","Prolactin-inhibiting hormone","Growth hormone-inhibitory hormone","Corticotropin-releasing hormone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Corticotropin-releasing hormone (CRH), a peptide, stimulates ACTH secretion by corticotropes. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which adrenal androgen precursors make up about 10% of adrenal cortical hormone output?","choices":["Aldosterone and DOC","Cortisol and corticosterone","Testosterone and estrogen","DHEA and androstenedione"],"answer":3,"exp":"DHEA and androstenedione make up approximately 10% of adrenal cortical hormone output. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Approximately 14 days after ovulation without fertilization, what happens to the endometrium?","choices":["It thickens indefinitely","Nothing changes","It sloughs off (menstruation)","It converts entirely into decidual tissue permanently"],"answer":2,"exp":"About 14 days after ovulation, if fertilization has not occurred, the endometrium sloughs off — menstruation. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Podocytes contribute to the filtration barrier through their footlike processes (pedicels), which are separated by:","choices":["Endothelial fenestrae pores lining the capillary wall","The surrounding mesangial matrix material","Filtration slits with a thin filtration diaphragm","Continuous tight junctions with no gaps"],"answer":2,"exp":"Podocyte pedicels are separated by filtration slits bridged by a thin filtration diaphragm containing pores that allow filtrate through while restricting larger molecules. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"ADH is secreted by which structure, and what is its primary site of action?","choices":["Secreted by the posterior pituitary; acts on the late distal tubule and collecting duct","Secreted by the adrenal cortex; acts on the thick ascending loop of Henle","Secreted by the kidney tissue itself; acts directly on the glomerulus","Secreted by the anterior pituitary gland; acts on the proximal tubule"],"answer":0,"exp":"ADH is secreted by the posterior pituitary and increases water permeability and reabsorption at the late distal tubule and collecting duct. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"The myosin molecule has two heavy chains and four light chains. Where is the site of ATPase activity located?","choices":["At the Z disc attachment point","In the head region of the molecule","Along the coiled tail region of the molecule","On the four light chains of the molecule"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the myosin head is the site of ATPase activity. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 23"},{"q":"Where do the nerve fibers carrying posterior pituitary hormones terminate?","choices":["At the adrenal medulla","At the thyroid gland directly","At capillaries in the neurohypophysis","At the anterior pituitary's portal vessels"],"answer":2,"exp":"Nerve fibers from the hypothalamus terminate at capillaries in the neurohypophysis (posterior pituitary), where hormones are released into the blood. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Solubility-wise, how does this lecture describe epinephrine (noting this specific slide's classification)?","choices":["Water soluble","Soluble only in the presence of CBG","Lipid soluble, like other adrenal cortex hormones","Neither lipid nor water soluble"],"answer":0,"exp":"This lecture's epinephrine profile slide lists it as water soluble. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Estrogen's physiological effects on the uterus include an increase in size by approximately:","choices":["1.5×","2–3×","No change in size","10×"],"answer":1,"exp":"Estrogen increases uterine size by approximately 2–3×, along with proliferation of endometrial stroma and glands. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which nerve, per the innervation summary table, controls the external sphincter and originates from S2–S4?","choices":["Vagus nerve","Pelvic nerve","Hypogastric nerve","Pudendal nerve"],"answer":3,"exp":"The pudendal nerve, originating from S2–S4, controls the external sphincter. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Reabsorption of water, glucose, amino acids, Na+, Cl-, and HCO3- occurs throughout the renal tubules but especially in which segment?","choices":["Loop of Henle exclusively","Distal convoluted tubule exclusively","Proximal convoluted tubule (PCT)","Collecting duct exclusively"],"answer":2,"exp":"Reabsorption of these substances occurs throughout the tubules but especially in the proximal convoluted tubule. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A single skeletal muscle fiber is best described as which of the following?","choices":["A bundle of contractile organelles bound by terminal cisternae","A multinucleated single cell enclosed by the sarcolemma","A single-nucleus cell enclosed by the sarcoplasm","A cluster of myofibrils surrounded by extracellular fluid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: muscle fibers are single, multinucleated cells surrounded by the sarcolemma. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 4"},{"q":"Growth hormone stimulates osteoblasts as part of its bone-growth actions. What is the functional role of osteoblasts?","choices":["Bone resorption","Bone formation/deposition","Red blood cell destruction","Fat storage within bone marrow"],"answer":1,"exp":"Osteoblasts are bone-forming cells, responsible for new bone deposition — consistent with GH's growth-promoting role. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"In the summary of glucose regulation, what is glucagon's role?","choices":["Functionally identical to insulin's glucose-lowering role","Increased by high glucose; lowers glucose further","Produced exclusively by hepatocytes within the liver","Increased by low glucose; returns glucose to normal"],"answer":3,"exp":"Glucagon is increased by low glucose and acts to return glucose to normal (i.e., raise low glucose). — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Estrogen's effect on skin and hair is described as:","choices":["Little impact on hair, but smooth and vascularized skin","Complete loss of skin vascularity","Strong stimulation of hair growth, similar to testosterone","Thickened, toughened skin with heavy sebaceous secretion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Estrogen has little impact on hair but softens the skin and makes it more vascularized. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"If a micturition reflex cycle occurs but fails to empty the bladder, what happens next according to the lecture?","choices":["The bladder immediately empties via a backup sympathetic reflex","The nervous elements remain inhibited for a period before the cycle can occur again","The nervous elements controlling it remain inhibited permanently","No further reflexes can occur until the next micturition event days later"],"answer":1,"exp":"Once a micturition reflex has occurred but not succeeded in emptying the bladder, the nervous elements controlling it remain inhibited for a period of time before another reflex occurs. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Per the summary table, the ascending limb of the loop of Henle reabsorbs which combination of water and NaCl?","choices":["None water, none NaCl","High water, high NaCl","None water, high NaCl","High water, low NaCl"],"answer":2,"exp":"The ascending limb reabsorbs none (no) water but high NaCl, consistent with its role in 'salt removal.' — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"In skeletal muscle excitation-contraction coupling, what is the role of the dihydropyridine (DHP) receptor?","choices":["It binds calcium for storage within the reticulum","It pumps calcium back into the reticulum","It acts as the voltage sensor in the T-tubule membrane","It is the calcium release channel of the reticulum"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: in skeletal muscle the dihydropyridine receptor is the voltage sensor that detects the action potential. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 17"}]};
